Quantitative investing leverages financial theories and statistical methods to efficiently extract and utilize various forms of information, enabling scientific portfolio management with the goal of achieving stable net asset growth.

This course is designed to help students understand the key concepts, principles, methodologies, and practical applications in the field of quantitative investing. The curriculum is structured into six modules:




Fundamentals of Quantitative Investing – covering the foundational framework of quantitative investing, classification and processing of investment data, and the logic and structure of backtesting strategies;




Quantitative Investment Theory – including the Markowitz portfolio selection framework, multi-factor pricing models, and related theoretical foundations;




Quantitative Statistical Models – introducing techniques such as linear regression and machine learning methods including deep learning;




Industry Guest Lectures – featuring invited practitioners sharing real-world insights and experiences;




Case Studies from AQR – analyzing classic cases from leading quantitative investment firm AQR Capital Management;




Frontiers in Quantitative Investing – exploring the latest research developments and engaging in academic literature review.

